ifconfig Command in Linux
Definition
c (Interface Configuration) is a Linux networking command used to display and configure network interface settings,
such as the IP address, subnet mask, MAC address, and network status.

Note: On modern Linux systems, ifconfig is deprecated. The ip command (e.g., ip addr) is now the recommended replacement.
Why do we use ifconfig?
View IP address.
Display network interface information.
Enable or disable a network interface.
Configure IP address and subnet mask.
Check MAC address.
Troubleshoot network issues.

ifconfig  Display all network interfaces
ifconfig eth0  Display a specific interface
